  "_style": "Standard Swahili (Kiswahili sanifu), warm and simple, never flowery or cheesy. Address the user as “wewe” (singular verb forms: unaweza, yako, kwako); address the two of them together with “nyinyi wawili” and second-person plural forms (mnaweza, wenu, -eni imperatives such as “Pandeni pamoja”); when the user speaks to the partner (messages, letters) write in first person “mimi/sisi” (nakuwaza, mti wetu). The partner is always “mwenzi wako” (never mpenzi/mke/mume/mchumba); Swahili pronouns and verb prefixes are gender-neutral (yeye, a-), so keep sentences neutral and never add gendered nouns like mwanamume/mwanamke. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never altered, inflected or given a prefix/suffix, and it must not force a noun-class agreement that could clash: rephrase (e.g. “%@ amepanda mbegu” is fine as subject; prefer “kutoka kwa %@”, “pamoja na %@”) or use “mwenzi wako” instead. Use curly quotes “ ” (inner ‘ ’), the single-character ellipsis …, no space before : ; ? !, keep “ · ”, emoji, \\n and every placeholder exactly as in the source. Numbers: comma for thousands, period for decimals (1,250.5); dates day–month–year with Swahili month names (14 Novemba 2026) and let system formatters do the work; use 24-hour clock unless the source formats it. Buttons and actions are short imperatives in the singular (Hifadhi, Tuma barua, Jaribu tena), no “tafadhali” on buttons; titles, tabs, chips and buttons use sentence case (capital on the first word only) except proper nouns, Coupley, Premium and AI. Latin script only. Plural categories are [\"one\",\"other\"]: “one” uses the singular noun form and “other” the plural (siku 1 / siku %lld; mti 1 / miti %lld; kumbukumbu, barua and picha do not change). Keep “Coupley” and “AI” untranslated.",
